course: dictionary
lesson: 8162
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Reservoir» is one of them, and it has three meanings. To find the right one, read the context: the words and sentences around it.
explain: «The clean water arrives from a treatment works, pushed along by pumps and by gravity from reservoirs and water towers up high.» Water is sent to homes from a store of water, so a reservoir here is a large lake that holds water for a town.
explain: «A lock needs no pump: water simply flows down from the higher part of the canal, and reservoirs keep the top of the canal full.» Reservoirs are stores of water, and the canal is kept full by them. Try your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense? If yes, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A reservoir is a large lake, often made by people with a dam, that stores water for a town or city.
fact: In a machine, a reservoir is a container or tank that holds liquid.
fact: Reservoir can also mean a large supply of something, such as «a reservoir of ideas».
fact: Reservoirs are deep and very cold, so never swim in them.
fact: The water cycle refills reservoirs.
exercise: «Never swim near dams or reservoirs; the water is cold and deep.» What does reservoir mean here? | A large supply of ideas | A small pond in a garden | A tank inside a machine | *A large lake made to store water
exercise: Jess says the reservoir in the machine is full of oil. What does reservoir mean here? | *A container or tank that holds liquid | A large lake with a dam | A big supply of ideas | A deep, cold place to swim
exercise: The author wrote, «She had a reservoir of ideas for her story.» What does reservoir mean? | A tank for fuel | A lock on a canal | A dam across a river | *A large supply of something
test: Which meaning of reservoir fits best in «Reservoirs keep the top of the canal full»? | A tank inside a machine | *A large lake that stores water | A large supply of ideas | A dry field
test: In «Never swim near dams or reservoirs», which clue helps you most? | Swim | Near | Never | *Dams, because a dam holds back water to make a lake
exam: Ben reads, «The reservoir was empty after the summer, so the town had to save water.» Which meaning is right? | A deep, safe place for boats | *A large lake that stores water for a town | A large supply of ideas | A tank inside a machine
